WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:04.791
Film Courage: I know we are what seems like
a freer culture right now where people don’t

00:00:04.791 --> 00:00:11.870
have to be bound by with as much as what their
own family of origin say that they should

00:00:11.870 --> 00:00:16.699
be doing and people seem to be okay with just
being who they are.

00:00:16.699 --> 00:00:19.150
It seems like we’re coming into our own
like that.

00:00:19.150 --> 00:00:22.029
I am just doing a bunch of double speak.

00:00:22.029 --> 00:00:27.271
Let’s talk about people who give you advice,
they are not in the industry and they are

00:00:27.271 --> 00:00:29.589
telling you “You shouldn’t do that.”

00:00:29.589 --> 00:00:38.060
But part of it may be that they won’t actually
allow themselves

00:00:38.060 --> 00:00:43.170
to go outside what their identity say they
should be.

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:46.040
Dar Dixon, Actor: The human condition baby,
right?

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:47.760
It happens all of the time.

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:50.880
I’m sure that you’ve felt it?

00:00:50.880 --> 00:01:00.700
Film Courage: Oh…I…yes…but I’ve never
been one to care about convention.

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:05.140
I can’t like a song that everyone else likes
until years later and then I hear it and go

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:06.140
“That’s pretty good?”

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:09.260
But because it was so popular at that time
I cringed.

00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:11.130
So I have that disease.

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:16.149
I know that this is its own affliction, maybe
it’s a contrarian complex, I don’t know

00:01:16.149 --> 00:01:17.170
what it is.

00:01:17.170 --> 00:01:21.469
Dar: It has to do with authority.

00:01:21.469 --> 00:01:27.020
So just think about who your primary authority
figures were in your life…psstt…Mom and

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:30.270
Dad and the relationship you had with them.

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:34.289
That’s where it all stems from.

00:01:34.289 --> 00:01:36.310
Anyway…yes they do.

00:01:36.310 --> 00:01:40.509
It takes courage to be an artist, it takes
enormous courage to be an artist.

00:01:40.509 --> 00:01:42.659
Whatever your art is.

00:01:42.659 --> 00:01:49.159
It’s not easy sitting down and pouring your
heart out into a script even if it’s a story

00:01:49.159 --> 00:01:50.159
you’ve created.

00:01:50.159 --> 00:01:54.509
It’s still your blood sweat and tears and
your heart on that page.

00:01:54.509 --> 00:01:56.049
That takes guts.

00:01:56.049 --> 00:02:01.310
To sit in front of a camera and act out those
words and perform it and bring it to life

00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:06.639
with a point of view incredibly hard, incredibly
hard.

00:02:06.639 --> 00:02:10.100
Same for the production, same for all of it.

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:12.560
It’s all challenging.

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:21.860
I salute all artists because it takes enormous
courage and that type of courage doesn’t

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:31.790
go unnoticed and when people who aren’t
chasing their dream or going after what they

00:02:31.790 --> 00:02:36.420
really want, whatever it may be, it doesn’t
necessarily mean they have to be in the arts,

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:37.510
whatever that may be.

00:02:37.510 --> 00:02:42.020
When they aren’t doing that and they have
fallen in step to conventional wisdom, to

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:48.690
groupthink (and I know a little bit about
that because I got caught up in that groupthink)

00:02:48.690 --> 00:02:55.240
that is extremely detrimental to one’s health,
happiness and safety.

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:59.140
And yes, jealousy comes out of that and condemnation.

00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:02.690
It’s the easiest thing in the world, we’ll
all pick and attack each other constantly

00:03:02.690 --> 00:03:06.000
instead of supporting each other and encouraging
each other.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:13.180
Then you see that the people who are the most
successful in any industry, in any walk of

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:19.270
life who are doing what they love, who love
what they’re doing, they are happier, they

00:03:19.270 --> 00:03:27.040
are more peaceful, they are the most generous,
the most supportive, the most loving, with

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:34.490
their time, their resources, their money,
whatever it may be because I think they see

00:03:34.490 --> 00:03:39.150
and they understand inherently how tough it
is to put yourself out on the line, to put

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:45.990
yourself out there, to let yourself be seen
(warts and all) which is going back to we

00:03:45.990 --> 00:03:48.750
all beat ourselves up “My gosh, what were
they thinking about me?

00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:49.750
Was that terrible?

00:03:49.750 --> 00:03:50.750
Was that awful?

00:03:50.750 --> 00:03:51.750
I’ll never work again.”

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:52.750
Right?

00:03:52.750 --> 00:03:53.750
Relax man, take it easy.

00:03:53.750 --> 00:03:54.750
It’s okay.

00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:55.960
I think it’s great, you go out and find
a mentor.

00:03:55.960 --> 00:04:00.400
Someone who is seeded and it doesn’t even
have to be the same thing you’re in.

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:08.040
I encourage everyone to go outside of their
comfort zone into completely unknown territories

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:12.000
and business and industries and start to learn
about it.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:15.830
If you have an interest in it not just willy
nilly “Hey I’m going to go into the auto

00:04:15.830 --> 00:04:19.100
manufacturing plant today.

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:25.960
If you’re interested in autos great but
just go put yourself out there whatever it

00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:27.040
may be.

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:36.740
Go to a seminar, go to a convention, go to
a meeting and see what’s going on and necessarily

00:04:36.740 --> 00:04:43.450
that starts to inform you really in the ways
you never expected.

00:04:43.450 --> 00:04:45.190
Really in the most wonderful ways.

00:04:45.190 --> 00:04:51.160
Film Courage: Have you ever had an audition
where it went horribly wrong and then it turns

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:53.020
out you got the part?

00:04:53.020 --> 00:04:54.560
And you were shocked.

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:58.430
Like you thought they didn’t like you, they
didn’t even look up from their papers?

00:04:58.430 --> 00:04:59.670
Some story like that?

00:04:59.670 --> 00:05:10.570
Dar: Only if we count every audition yes that’s
happened quite a few times actually.

00:05:10.570 --> 00:05:15.670
And that’s one of those times where I stop
and thought to myself “Son you don’t know

00:05:15.670 --> 00:05:17.889
anything.

00:05:17.889 --> 00:05:26.970
You have got preconceived ideas, presuppositions,
you’re just like…maybe you should rethink

00:05:26.970 --> 00:05:29.669
a few things.”

00:05:29.669 --> 00:05:39.090
And that’s a good thing because it takes
you outside of yourself and that’s not what

00:05:39.090 --> 00:05:40.930
everyone else is doing.

00:05:40.930 --> 00:05:45.370
You can live your whole life very comfortably
doing what you’re suppose to do: go to school,

00:05:45.370 --> 00:05:49.052
get good grades, graduate, get your job, buy
a house, get your mortgage, have kids, get

00:05:49.052 --> 00:05:55.730
a family and that’s all wonderful and if
it’s not your thing then it’s not your

00:05:55.730 --> 00:06:02.389
thing and you need to find out what your thing
is and then go and do that.

00:06:02.389 --> 00:06:08.680
And doing that is going to necessarily bring
up the thing you’re talking about.

00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:15.980
People judging you or people being jealous
of you or trying to pull you down or people

00:06:15.980 --> 00:06:22.520
trying to make it hard for you in any number
of ways usually it’s passive-aggressiveness

00:06:22.520 --> 00:06:28.850
but you’ve got to find that strength within
yourself and just keep soldiering on.

00:06:28.850 --> 00:06:35.530
Film Courage: We all here these phrases “Fear
is the absence of love.”

00:06:35.530 --> 00:06:41.590
Do you think when you actually see it happen
and it could be from people whom you went

00:06:41.590 --> 00:06:48.139
to school with that you were close to in college
and then you go on a slightly different path

00:06:48.139 --> 00:06:49.770
and you see their fear reaction.

00:06:49.770 --> 00:06:56.570
And then it becomes lonely because it becomes
do I comply and stay with the group and now

00:06:56.570 --> 00:07:01.720
I have someone where to go or do I do what
I want to do and then maybe the phone stops

00:07:01.720 --> 00:07:02.720
ringing.

00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:06.850
Dar: Yes, that’s crazy town right there.

00:07:06.850 --> 00:07:11.720
Film Courage: It can be the truth for a lot
of people.

00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:14.440
It can be true for entrepreneurs who want
to start a business and people say “You

00:07:14.440 --> 00:07:15.440
are ruining your life.

00:07:15.440 --> 00:07:17.030
Don’t leave that good job.”

00:07:17.030 --> 00:07:19.640
Dar: That’s a great point and yes all of
that happens.

00:07:19.640 --> 00:07:28.139
It has happened but it happens to me a lot
less now but that’s because I made a concerted

00:07:28.139 --> 00:07:37.169
effort to really keep myself more focused,
more aware of what I’m doing and what’s

00:07:37.169 --> 00:07:39.540
going on in any given situation that I’m
in.

00:07:39.540 --> 00:07:46.570
Like I said awareness creates choice, the
more choice I have I’m always going to naturally

00:07:46.570 --> 00:07:52.130
choose what serves me and the situation better.

00:07:52.130 --> 00:07:56.250
So entrepreneurs and artists are really kind
of one in the same thing because they all

00:07:56.250 --> 00:07:57.400
have the same struggle.

00:07:57.400 --> 00:08:03.901
You are creating something whatever that may
be and you’re going to fail more often than

00:08:03.901 --> 00:08:05.229
you succeed.

00:08:05.229 --> 00:08:08.290
That’s just how it’s going to go.

00:08:08.290 --> 00:08:12.110
And that requires a lot of courage and you
are going to have to deal with a lot of doubts,

00:08:12.110 --> 00:08:21.620
a lot of fears and a lot of resentments and
jealousies and and and…And you have to find

00:08:21.620 --> 00:08:24.490
your way through that because there is no
one way.

00:08:24.490 --> 00:08:28.650
If anyone tries to tell you “This is the
way you do it” is either lying to you or

00:08:28.650 --> 00:08:32.260
bull talking you or trying to sell you something.”

00:08:32.260 --> 00:08:33.260
Really simple right?

00:08:33.260 --> 00:08:34.260
Film Courage: Or all three.

00:08:34.260 --> 00:08:41.270
Dar: Or all three at the same time which is
an especially distasteful combination.

00:08:41.270 --> 00:08:48.110
So…be nice to yourself man, find out what
works for you.

00:08:48.110 --> 00:08:51.920
And you are going to find out what works for
you by finding out what doesn’t work for

00:08:51.920 --> 00:08:53.410
you constantly.

00:08:53.410 --> 00:08:58.050
Fear, everyone has it.

00:08:58.050 --> 00:09:05.360
Brene Brown became very popular with her TED
Talk and then her appearances on national

00:09:05.360 --> 00:09:12.510
television, it’s genius and it’s simplicity
and it’s showing your vulnerability.

00:09:12.510 --> 00:09:17.880
Look I don’t have the answers, I certainly
don’t in fact the more I learn the more

00:09:17.880 --> 00:09:24.140
I learn I don’t know anything in the grand
scheme of things.

00:09:24.140 --> 00:09:28.700
When you show yourself right, when you put
yourself out there, when you let yourself

00:09:28.700 --> 00:09:38.240
be seen, your work, your artwork, your direction
(whatever it may be) your music, your lyrics,

00:09:38.240 --> 00:09:46.480
your dancing, your body, your hair, everything
that’s necessarily a vulnerable place but

00:09:46.480 --> 00:09:54.770
that’s where all the magic happens, letting
people see you and feel your humanity and

00:09:54.770 --> 00:10:00.410
see it for all of its flaws because we all
have them, we all do and in this industry

00:10:00.410 --> 00:10:04.940
it’s really easy to put forth an image and
have a narrative and then get that thing stamped

00:10:04.940 --> 00:10:10.500
boom boom boom boom across all of these television
shows and all of these radio interviews and

00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:18.180
all of these podcasts or you are very pensive
or whatever it may be but that’s not the

00:10:18.180 --> 00:10:22.690
whole picture, it never is, it’s just in
the frame.

00:10:22.690 --> 00:10:26.680
And what’s in the frame is in the frame
but that’s not the whole story.

00:10:26.680 --> 00:10:31.560
So don’t be afraid to let someone know you
don’t know what’s going on.

00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:33.730
Or “You know what, I’m scared man.

00:10:33.730 --> 00:10:35.870
I don’t know how this is going to go.”

00:10:35.870 --> 00:10:41.250
That happens where I go and do a scene and
it’s a very emotional scene or a very dangerous

00:10:41.250 --> 00:10:49.370
scene or a very psychical scene “I hope
this goes well.”

00:10:49.370 --> 00:10:55.550
But I’ve done my work, I’m made strong
choices and I know what the story being told

00:10:55.550 --> 00:11:01.150
is and I know what’s going on and then I’ve
got to trust the other actor and the writing

00:11:01.150 --> 00:11:05.380
and words on the page and the setting and
the dynamic and just go.

00:11:05.380 --> 00:11:07.040
Let it happen right?

00:11:07.040 --> 00:11:11.380
Similarly I think it would be great advice
to add that into your life as well and great

00:11:11.380 --> 00:11:16.170
advice to start to add that into your life
as well kind of coming back full circle “art

00:11:16.170 --> 00:11:17.170
saved my life.”

00:11:17.170 --> 00:11:18.200
I learned that through art.

00:11:18.200 --> 00:11:19.529
Question For The Viewers: What does this conversation
make you think about?

